      "dom_id": "question-214598",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4).$ Express the result as an inequality.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty ,4]$ and $(2,4)$ separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248994/assets/6ea8f23ba164e820b4ae33f6dbd7e5d70e9dd0b5af052a7ce5ecdcb680d3a895.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248994/assets/81f94d814f98021781460cbe7c30470e3021832b40525da36563bb11af9804ad.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248994/assets/772ae637383243d60d40f9bc015c5347b92159b7eccb27cefaa661341176b9d6.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4)=(2,4).$ This corresponds to the inequality $2<x<4.$",
